# Unions and never

### Every kind has a case, or it does not compile

Week 2, Rung 1 of 7: Seed. Talk 1

---

## The problem

A Tutors course has nine kinds in rung 1, and nineteen upstream. Each kind must be handled in several places:

| Place | Rung |
| --- | --- |
| how the folder is read | 1, `buildLo` |
| where it sorts among its siblings | 1, `preOrder` |
| how it looks as a card, which pages it gets | 2, `card` and `pagesFor` (week 3) |
| how the reader shows it | 5, the reader's components (weeks 8-9) |

Add a kind and forget one place, and the course is quietly wrong. Nobody is told.

---

## How upstream types a learning object

```ts
// packages/jsr/model/src/types/learning-objects.ts (tutors-mono-repo 2b96b69)
export type Lo = {
  type: string;
  id: string;
  title: string;
  ...
};
```

`type` is any `string`. So in `packages/jsr/gen/src/services/course-builder.ts`:

```ts
function buildSimpleLo(lo: Lo, lr: LearningResource): Lo {
  switch (lo.type) {
    case "lab": ...
    case "github": ...
    default:          // a new kind lands here, and nothing happens
  }
```

and the sort reads `preOrder.get(a.type) ?? Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`. Both are L0: the rule is implied.

---

## A discriminated union

```ts
// variants/rung-1-seed/src/model.ts
type LoBase = { id: string; title: string; summary: string; contentMd: string; route: string };
type CompositeBase = LoBase & { los: Lo[] };

export type Course = CompositeBase & { type: "course" };
export type Topic  = CompositeBase & { type: "topic" };
export type Talk   = LoBase & { type: "talk" };
export type Web    = LoBase & { type: "web" };
export type Archive = LoBase & { type: "archive"; archiveFile: string };
export type Lab    = LoBase & { type: "lab"; los: LabStep[] };
...
export type Lo = Course | Topic | Unit | Side | Talk | Lab | Note | Web | Archive;
export type LoType = Lo["type"];   // "course" | "topic" | ... | "archive"
```

Each member has a **literal** `type`. That one field tells TypeScript which shape it holds.

---

## Narrowing

```ts
function stepTitles(lo: Lo): string[] {
  if (lo.type === "lab") return lo.los.map((step) => step.title);  // lo is Lab here
  return [];
}

function zipOf(lo: Lo): string {
  return lo.archiveFile;
  //        ~~~~~~~~~~~ Property 'archiveFile' does not exist on type 'Lo'.
  //                    Property 'archiveFile' does not exist on type 'Course'.
}
```

After a check on `type`, the compiler knows the rest of the shape. Before it, the compiler refuses to guess.
The test in `tests/course-builder.test.ts` uses the same move: `assert.ok(topic.type === "topic")`.

---

## The exhaustive switch

```ts
// variants/rung-1-seed/src/course-builder.ts
export function buildLo(root: string, rel: string, kind: LoType): Lo {
  const folder = readFolder(root, rel);
  switch (kind) {
    case "course":  ...
    case "topic":   ...
    case "unit":
    case "side":    ...
    case "talk":    ...
    case "note":    ...
    case "web":     ...
    case "archive": ...
    case "lab":     return buildLab(folder);
    default:
      return assertNever(kind);   // kind is `never` here, unless a case is missing
  }
}

export function assertNever(value: never): never {
  throw new Error(`unhandled learning object kind: ${String(value)}`);
}
```

---

## Add a kind, and read what the compiler says

Add `export type Github = LoBase & { type: "github" }` and put `Github` in the `Lo` union. Then:

```
$ npx tsc -p .
src/course-builder.ts(122,26): error TS2345: Argument of type '"github"'
  is not assignable to parameter of type 'never'.
src/model.ts(50,14): error TS2741: Property 'github' is missing in type
  '{ course: number; unit: number; ... archive: number; }' but required in type
  'Record<"course" | "topic" | ... | "github" | "archive" | "lab", number>'.
```

Two places, named by file and line. That is **L5, Unrepresentable Mistake**: a `Lo` the builder cannot
build is not a program that compiles.

---

## The second demand: `Record<LoType, number>`

```ts
// variants/rung-1-seed/src/model.ts
export const preOrder: Record<LoType, number> = {
  course: 0, unit: 1, side: 2, topic: 3, talk: 4, lab: 7, note: 8, web: 9, archive: 11,
};
```

A `Record` keyed by the union needs **every** key. Upstream's `preOrder` is a `Map<string, number>`, so a
missing kind is `undefined`, and the sort quietly puts it last.

Prefer a type that cannot be incomplete over a default that hides it.

---

## What the compiler does not demand

```ts
export const folderKinds: [prefix: string, kind: LoType | null][] = [
  ...
  ["github", null],     // still compiles after you add "github" to Lo
];
```

The prefix table is **data**. `null` is a valid value, so TypeScript is satisfied, and every `github-`
folder is still skipped.

L5 covers the shape. The mapping needs L3: a test that builds a `github-` folder and expects a `github`
object. The assignment's grading test is that test. Pick the weakest level that covers each risk.

---

## Why `assertNever` also throws

Rung 1 runs TypeScript with Node's **type stripping**: `node src/main.ts`. Node removes the types and runs
the JavaScript. It never checks them.

- your editor runs the checker as you type, with `tsconfig.json`'s strict settings
- the gate gains `tsc --noEmit` only in rung 3 (week 4), when TypeScript becomes a dev dependency
- until then, a kind that slips through fails **loudly** at run time:

```ts
test("a kind that slips past the type checker fails loudly at run time", () => {
  assert.throws(() => assertNever("podcast" as never), /unhandled learning object kind: podcast/);
});
```

A wrong `tutors.json` written quietly is worse than a crash.

---

## Erasable syntax only

Type stripping can only **erase** types; it cannot generate code. So rung 1 uses none of:

| Not allowed | Use instead |
| --- | --- |
| `enum Kind { Talk, Lab }` | a union of string literals: `"talk" \| "lab"` |
| `namespace Model { ... }` | a module: `src/model.ts` |
| `constructor(private fs: Fs)` | a field and an assignment |
| `import { Lo } from "./model.ts"` for a type | `import type { Lo } from "./model.ts"` |

`tsconfig.json` sets `erasableSyntaxOnly` and `verbatimModuleSyntax`, so your editor flags each one.

---

## See L5 on rung 1 today

Rung 1 installs nothing, and its fence forbids dependencies in `package.json`. You can still run the
checker without saving it:

```bash
cd variants/rung-1-seed
npm install --no-save typescript@6 @types/node@22
npx tsc -p .
```

`--no-save` leaves `package.json` alone, so the Rung Fence stays green. `node_modules/` is in `.gitignore`.
The lab does this, then breaks the switch on purpose.

---

## Upstream is moving the same way

```ts
// packages/jsr/types/src/learning-objects.ts (tutors-mono-repo 2b96b69)
// Each closed vocabulary is one `as const` list with its type derived from it, so adding a kind
// is one edit and every `switch` over the type can be checked for exhaustiveness.
export const LO_SIMPLE_TYPES = ["note", "archive", "web", "github", ... , "quiz"] as const;
export const LO_COMPOSITE_TYPES = ["unit", "side", "topic", "course"] as const;
export const LO_TYPES = [...LO_SIMPLE_TYPES, ...LO_COMPOSITE_TYPES] as const;
/** A learning-object kind, such as "lab" or "topic". Model-lib's `Lo.type` is still `string`. */
export type LoType = (typeof LO_TYPES)[number];
```

Its README says nothing imports it yet. Moving `Lo.type` onto it is real upstream work, and rung 1 shows
what it buys.

---

## Summary

- A discriminated union gives every learning object a literal `type`
- `switch` plus `assertNever`, and `Record<LoType, ...>`, make a forgotten kind a compile error (L5)
- data tables such as `folderKinds` still need a test (L3)
- type stripping never checks types, so the guard also throws at run time

Next: how rung 1 proves it agrees with upstream, and what keeps it on rung 1.
